   "source": [
    "## Questions\n",
    "* Why the phase-sensitive probes are useful: To proof the generalisability of the teleportation to multiple bases (X, Y and Z), the probes must be phase-sensitive to show that the teleportation (in the noiseless case) is not only sound in terms of bits, but in correctly transmitting phase as well.\n",
    "\n",
    "* Why Bob needs Alice’s outcomes: Alice applies a Hadamard measurement on her two qubits after introducing some entanglement with Bob's qubit, causing Bob's qubit to deviate from the original unknown qubit, meaning Bob has to correct this perturbation in order to accurately \"receive\" the qubit from the teleportation. Thus, Alice must supply Bob with her measurement.\n",
    "\n",
    "* Why readout errors can affect corrections: Because Bob relies on Alice's readouts to correct, if the readout induces an error, it can cause Bob to correct his qubit wrongly. This may introduce a mismatch between the final state of Bob's qubit and the state of the original unknown qubit, meaning the teleportation fails."
   ]
  },
